## 3. RULING on disclosure (c) — the three assertions added after the red

  **The builder's argument holds, and I did not take it on argument. I measured it.**

  The facts first, independently confirmed: `git diff --stat 1a5f490 HEAD` on the test
  file shows 108 insertions landing in the implementation commit 86cd26f, after
  `07.log` was recorded. The three blocks are `joined-budget`, `result-gate` and
  `own-stderr` — the gaps the builder's own round-3 mutation battery exposed.

  The question is whether a red was available for them and skipped. That is
  answerable by experiment, so I ran it: the CURRENT test file against the PRE-FIX
  implementation (talk.ts and claude-code.ts at 300aa85), behaviour 7.

    four assertions FAIL — all of them the ones already in 07.log:
      claude-model: carries the envelope's own result sentence
      claude-model: carries the vendor's stderr line
      spawn-claude: carries a substring of the spawn error
      spawn-codex:  carries a substring of the spawn error
    all EIGHTEEN assertions in the three post-red blocks PASS:
      joined-budget  6/6 PASS      result-gate  6/6 PASS      own-stderr  6/6 PASS

  Not one of them could have distinguished red from green. The reason is structural,
  and it is the builder's reason: round 2's `vendorDiagnostic` reads `stderr` then
  falls back to `stdout` and does nothing else — it never reads `result`, never reads
  `error`, never joins parts. So every one of these assertions is *vacuously*
  satisfied pre-fix. `result-gate` asserts a `result` key does NOT leak when
  `is_error` is false: pre-fix nothing reads `result` at all, so it trivially holds.
  `joined-budget` asserts the second part is cut: pre-fix there is no second part.
  `own-stderr` asserts the capture guard does not overwrite the envelope's own
  `stderr`: pre-fix there is no capture guard, and `raw: parsed` already surfaces the
  envelope's own key.

  So the ruling: **these are guard assertions against over-reach in a mechanism that
  did not exist pre-fix, and a mechanism that does not exist cannot be gripped.**
  Demanding a red for them would demand a fabricated one — the W-044 round-3
  provenance fault the brief already names, and the same reasoning the brief itself
  used to keep `talk-boundary.test.ts`'s hardening unnumbered. Their evidence is not
  a red; it is the mutation run, and it has to be, because their whole subject is
  "the fix must not do more than it was asked to". I re-ran that evidence myself
  (§4: M1, M7 and M8 all CAUGHT), so the assertions demonstrably bite.

  Two things I credit specifically. The builder found these three by mutating its own
  finished work, which is the discipline that catches what a spec did not think of —
  and the brief's scenario table genuinely did not think of them. And it disclosed
  them as gaps in the brief rather than presenting them as planned coverage, which is
  what let me check the question rather than discover it.

  One qualification, not a finding: the argument I verified is *specific to these
  three*. "The pre-fix code cannot distinguish it" is a sound reason to skip a red,
  and a dangerous one to generalise, because it is exactly what someone would say
  about an assertion that simply fits the implementation. What made it checkable here
  is that the property is negative and the pre-fix mechanism is absent — both facts a
  censor can test in one run, as above. Worth writing into the next brief that uses
  the argument: state it as "this assertion passes against the pre-fix tree, here is
  the run", not as a claim about intent.

