## 5. F-4 — CLOSED. Verified against the live text, the live vendor, and the mutations

  The widened read is one function at one site, exactly as specced
  (`vendorDiagnostic`, packages/commands/src/talk.ts): collects `stderr`, then
  `result` **gated on `is_error === true`**, then `error` (string, or an object's
  `.message`); falls back to `stdout` alone when nothing else is present; joins with
  " · "; redacts; truncates to the ONE existing 300-character budget; prefixes
  `status <value>: ` when `is_error` and `api_error_status` are both present. Nothing
  moved into a profile. The one profile change is a capture fix, not a second reader:
  `claude-code.ts`'s parsed-envelope return now carries `{ ...parsed, stderr }` only
  when stderr is non-empty and the envelope has no `stderr` key of its own.

  I checked the two things most likely to be got wrong and both are right: redaction
  happens BEFORE truncation (so a secret cannot be cut in half and slip past the
  pattern — round 2's order was the other way and this is an improvement), and the
  `status` prefix is added after truncation, i.e. deliberately outside the budget,
  which is what the spec says.

  ### The live probe (pre-authorized), verbatim

    npm run -s bisellium -- talk --sella art-lead --model-only \
      --studio <copy of examples/sample-studio> "Name the opus you are booted for and stop."

    EXIT 1, and the message — quoted, not paraphrased:

      art-lead: claude-code exited 1 (requested model: gpt-6-astra) — status 401:
      [claude-code:unrecognized_model] {"model":"gpt-6-astra","query_source":"sdk"} ·
      Failed to authenticate. API Error: 401 OAuth access token has expired.
      Re-authenticate to continue.

    No session, timeline or receipt written.

  Every element of the widened read is visible in one line: the `api_error_status`
  prefix (`status 401:`), the vendor's stderr line — which only reaches the operator
  because of the `claude-code.ts` capture fix, since the envelope parsed — the ` · `
  join, and the envelope's own `result` text gated on `is_error`. Round 1's message
  for this same case was `art-lead: claude-code exited 1`. Round 2's added the model.
  This one tells the operator what actually happened.

  It is in fact **stronger evidence than the probe I asked for**: the vendor returned
  a 401 auth failure rather than the 404 model error I measured in round 2 (the
  operator's OAuth token expired during this session — see O-14), so the read was
  exercised against a raw shape nobody wrote a stub for, and carried both parts
  correctly anyway. The mechanism generalises rather than fitting one measured case.

  Honest qualification, O-15: because the vendor answered 401, the specific sentence
  "There's an issue with the selected model…" was not itself observed reaching the
  operator live this round. It is pinned by scenario A's stub against the shape I
  measured in round 2, and mutation M2 proves that assertion bites.

  ### The three instances

  I-1 (parsed `is_error` envelope, claude-code.ts:149) — closed by the capture fix
       plus the `result`/`api_error_status` reads. Live-proven above; M6 and M2 bite.
  I-2 (claude spawn failure, claude-code.ts:131) — closed by the `error` read.
       Scenario D; M3 bites.
  I-3 (codex spawn failure, codex.ts:246) — same read, same site. Scenario E; M3 and
       M4 bite. `codex.ts` is untouched this round and correctly so: both its
       non-zero returns already carried `stderr`, and only the spawn-error shape was
       ever unread.

  All three in one change, as the amendment required and as my round-2 log asked for.
  The W-026 pattern — one instance per round — did not happen here.

  ### Disclosure (a), verified rather than accepted

  Scenarios D and E do not stub a spawn failure; they wrap the REAL profiles and force
  `env.PATH` at a directory with no binary, through `runTalk`'s existing `harnesses`
  seam. The builder's stated reason is sound and I confirmed it holds: PATH resolution
  precedes argv inspection, so no stub file can make `available()` succeed while the
  turn's spawn fails — there is no such shape. Evidence that the wrapper reaches real
  profile code rather than a synthetic object: the assertions match on `ENOENT`, which
  only a real `spawnSync`/`child.on("error")` failure produces, and M3 (dropping the
  `error` read at the shared site) fails both D and E — so both are genuinely routed
  through the real `Turn.raw = { error }` shape into the real `performTalk` pipeline.

