  O-16 Scenarios D/E's wrapper is a legitimate use of the `harnesses` test seam and
       runs real profile code (verified, §5). Worth knowing it exists: it is the only
       way this suite can reach the spawn-failure shape, and a future reader might
       otherwise mistake it for a mock.
  O-17 Three of behaviour 7's assertions (`result-gate`, `own-stderr`, the synthetic
       half of `joined-budget`) drive `Turn.raw` shapes no current profile produces.
       That is deliberate and right — F-1's lesson is that omission today is not
       safety tomorrow — but it means those three are guards against future vendor or
       profile change, not descriptions of present behaviour. A reader pruning "dead"
       assertions later should leave them alone; the comments say why.
  O-18 Round 2's O-11 is addressed and I record it closed: the round-3 amendment
       commit 99d3d0c carries `Co-Authored-By: architect` alone, and the brief now
       states in its own header that it is signed by the architect alone, naming the
       round-2 trailer as the orchestrator's error. The correction is in the record
       rather than silently applied, which is the right way to close it.

  The disclosure class opened in round 1's O-2 (hand-corrected evidence, disclosed by
  memory) has now improved across three rounds: nowhere durable -> commit message ->
  traditio plus commit message plus the brief's own text, with the reason named and
  the authorizing verdict cited. I am not filing it as unaddressed. The standing
  proposal for the retro is unchanged and remains worth doing — `verify`/`red` should
  record or refuse rather than rely on an agent remembering — but the practice here
  no longer depends on it.

## 8. Verdict

  PASS, round 3.

  F-4 is closed as a class, not an instance: all three `Turn.raw` shapes in one
  change, one reader at one site, the capture fix confined to the one profile that
  was discarding a stream it already held. Ten of ten mutations bite, including the
  three gaps the builder found in the brief's own scenario table and disclosed rather
  than absorbed. The re-recorded `07.log` was authorized, is disclosed in the
  traditio, and reproduces assertion-for-assertion from a tree I rebuilt myself. The
  three post-red assertions are rightly unred, and I say so on the strength of a run
  rather than an argument.

  And the whole thing is visible in one live line: a message that read
  `art-lead: claude-code exited 1` at round 1 now tells the operator the status code,
  the vendor's error identifier and the vendor's own sentence about what went wrong.

  Two things the next opus inherits, both already written into the brief rather than
  left here: the egress residual and its follow-on question, and the rule this opus
  cost three rounds to learn — a claim enters a shipped document only with an
  assertion that fails when it stops being true.
